INSERT INTO `team_run_events` (`id`, `run_id`, `seq`, `event_name`, `agent_key`, `parent_agent_key`, `task_id`, `tool_call_id`, `payload`, `created_at`) VALUES
(1453, '40122540-fcbb-4890-9b07-1296c60efc8f', 190465, 'agent.turn.completed', 'code', 'moonya', 'pages_study', NULL, '{\"turn_id\":\"pages_study-turn-56\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"reasoning_content\":\"Now jsdom runs, and there are real failures to analyze. But wait — many failures suggest elements not found (e.g., \\\"错题原因弹窗缺失\\\", \\\"批量导入弹窗缺失\\\", \\\"对比柱状图缺失\\\"). These could be real product bugs OR test-order issues. Let me analyze carefully:\\n\\n**Suspicious pattern**: The failures cascade — e.g., tree selection failed, then everything after... Actually let me look at each:\\n\\n1. `tree: 选择图形推理后应显示 1 个卡片，实际 2` — After clicking `[data-select=\\\"行测/判断推理/图形推理\\\"]`, expected 1 card (k1) but got 2. Hmm — wait, the tree initial render selected [\'行测\'] → 2 cards (k1, k2). Clicking node \'行测/判断推理/图形推理\' should re-render list with 1 card. Got 2 → click didn\'t work?\\n\\nWhy? The click: `click(el, \'[data-select=\\\"行测/判断推理/图形推理\\\"]\')` → el.querySelector finds the button → node.click() → event delegation on treePane. The handler: `var sel = e.target.closest(\'[data-select]\')` — target is the button itself → closest → found. Then `ts2.selected = key` where key = sel.dataset.select.split(\'/\') = [\'行测\',\'判断推理\',\'图形推理\'] — wait, dataset.select contains \'行测/判断推理/图形推理\' — split(\'/\') gives [\'行测\',\'判断推理\',\'图形推理\'] ✓. setUI({...}) → persists. refreshTree(); refreshList() → refreshList reads treeState() → getUI().knowledgeTree.selected — should be the new selection...\\n\\nHold on — `setUI({ knowledgeTree: { selected: key, expanded: ts2.expanded } })` — setUI does `App.store.updateSettings({ studyUI: Object.assign({}, cur, patch) })`. cur = settings.studyUI = {} initially. So studyUI = { knowledgeTree: {...} } ✓.\\n\\nBut wait — in TREE_DEF.render, treeState() reads `getUI().knowledgeTree` — fine. In refreshList, `var ts = treeState();` — reads again ✓. Then knowledgeForNode(ts.selected) — selected = [\'行测\',\'判断推理\',\'图形推理\'] → k1 only. Expected 1... but got 2.\\n\\nHmm, unless the click never happened because `el.querySelector(\'[data-select=\\\"行测/判断推理/图形推理\\\"]\')` returns null? In jsdom, attribute selector with slashes works. Actually wait — the tree render: nodeHTML generates `data-select=\\\"${key}\\\"` where key=\'行测/判断推理/图形推理\'. OK.\\n\\nOR the problem: `click()` helper — `node.click()` on a <button> inside treePane — the handler is attached to treePane (parent). click() dispatches click event that bubbles ✓.\\n\\nHmm wait — but the FIRST failure is actually that... let me re-check: maybe the click DID work but `refreshList` uses `search.value` — search input exists. items = knowledgeForNode([\'行测\',\'判断推理\',\'图形推理\']) → k1 ✓ 1 card. But test says 2.\\n\\nUnless... treeState() after setUI: `App.store.updateSettings` in my stub: `settings = Object.assign({}, settings, patch)` — replaces settings object → settings.studyUI = { knowledgeTree: {...} }. getUI reads `App.store.settings.studyUI` — store.settings is the CLOSURE variable `settings`? No! In harness3, `store = { settings: settings, updateSettings: function(patch){ settings = Object.assign({}, settings, patch); } ...}` — store.settings references the original `settings` object. After updateSettings, `settings` variable is reassigned to a NEW object, but `store.settings` still points to the OLD object! So getUI() reads store.settings.studyUI → undefined → treeState defaults to [\'行测\'] → list shows 2 cards!\\n\\nTHAT\'S A HARNESS BUG: store.settings must be kept in sync. In real store.js, `settings: state.settings` and updateSettings mutates `state.settings = Object.assign(...)` — reassigning state.settings, so store.settings reads fresh each time (it\'s a property access on the store object → state.settings at call time). In my stub, `settings:` captured the value once. Fix stub: make store.settings a getter:\\n\\n```js\\nvar settingsStore = { theme:\'light\', fontSize:15, sidebarCollapsed:false, studyUI:{}, studyClock:{} };\\nvar store = {\\n  get settings(){ return settingsStore; },\\n  updateSettings: function(patch){ settingsStore = Object.assign({}, settingsStore, patch); },\\n  ...\\n};\\n```\\nThat matches real behavior.\\n\\nThis explains many cascading failures: tree (2 cards), clock (initial timer 00:00:00 — because settings.studyClock set before mount... wait clock failure: \\\"初始计时显示错误: 00:00:00\\\" — I set `settings.studyClock = {...}` — but the test sets the harness variable `settings`, while store.settings still points to old object → clockState reads old (empty) → elapsed 0 → 00:00:00. Yes! Same root cause.\\n\\nAnd sz month persistence failures — same root cause (setUI writes to new object, reads via store.settings old → studyUI always {}). Also detail review date failure: `App.store.knowledge.update(\'k1\', {mastery:5, reviewDate})` → k1 update worked (update mutates arr) but reviewDate assertion: expected daysFromNow(14)?? Wait — my assertion: `if (!k1.reviewDate || k1.reviewDate !== daysFromNow(14))` — EB_DAYS[4]=15 → reviewDateForLevel(5) = today+15 days! My assertion says 14 — TEST BUG. EB_DAYS = [1,2,4,7,15]; level 5 → EB_DAYS[4]=15 → +15 days. Test asserted +14. Fix assertion to daysFromNow(15). Hmm wait, actually let me double check: mastery modal onConfirm: `var reviewDate = reviewDateForLevel(level);` level=5 → reviewDateForLevel(5) → EB_DAYS[5-1]=EB_DAYS[4]=15 → +15 days. And the toast says fmtMD. The assertion in the harness expected daysFromNow(14) — wrong; should be 15. Test bug.\\n\\n2. `tree: 折叠展开切换无效` — toggle: click \'[data-toggle=\\\"行测\\\"]\' → handler `e.target.closest(\'[data-toggle]\')` → toggles ts.expanded[\'行测\'] → setUI → refreshTree. But refreshTree reads treeState from store.settings (old object) → unchanged → openBefore===openAfter... same root cause. Will pass after stub fix. Also note: after toggling, `node` reference — I query `el.querySelector(\'.st-node[data-key=\\\"行测\\\"]\')` before click and check classList after refreshTree (innerHTML replaced → node is STALE). So after refreshTree, the old node reference no longer in DOM — its classList won\'t change! Test bug: must re-query after click. Fix: re-query node after click.\\n\\n3. `questions: 卡片展开失败` — click toggle: `el.querySelector(\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')` — the toggle button is inside `.st-q-extra[hidden]`! With hidden attribute, jsdom still allows click (click on hidden element works programmatically). But `node.click()` dispatches event — handler on listBox: `e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\')` → toggle → `btn.closest(\'.st-q-card\')` → toggles expanded ✓. Hmm why fail? `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')` after click → should be true...\\n\\nWait — but the failure list shows `questions: 卡片展开失败` AND `错题原因弹窗缺失` AND `批量导入弹窗缺失` — modalState.html checks. Let me think about what could break mount for questions page. `mountPage(\'/study/questions\')` → render → content.innerHTML = html → mount(content, path). In mount: `listBox = el.querySelector(\'#st-q-list\')` ✓. renderList() called at end ✓ (cards created). Click toggle... \\n\\nHmm wait, actually — did mount even run correctly? The FIRST failure was tree — after tree test, we clicked `[data-select=...]` which calls setUI → updateSettings → settings reassigned (stub bug) — that doesn\'t break questions mount.\\n\\nLet me think about `questions: 卡片展开失败` more carefully. In jsdom, `node.click()` — the click event target = the button. Handler: `var btn = e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\')` — btn.dataset.action = \'toggle\' ✓. `var c = btn.closest(\'.st-q-card\'); if (c) toggleCard(c);` → c.classList.toggle(\'expanded\') ✓. Then test checks `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')` where card1 = `el.querySelector(\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"]\')` — same element ✓...\\n\\nUnless the click threw before toggling. What\'s in toggleCard? `var ex = card.querySelector(\'.st-q-extra\'); if (ex) ex.hidden = !expanded;` fine.\\n\\nHmm wait — maybe the issue is event listener binding: listBox.addEventListener(\'click\', ...) — but at mount, `renderList()` was called BEFORE... no, the listener is added before renderList()? Let me check the order in QUESTIONS_DEF.mount: listeners bound (listBox click, filter changes, add, import) then `renderList();` at the end. OK.\\n\\nBUT — there\'s the `focusId` part: focusId=\'\' initially — skip.\\n\\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe the problem is that mount ran on a page whose innerHTML includes `<svg>` from icon() — fine.\\n\\nActually — wait. Let me recheck `click()` helper: it does `el.querySelector(sel)` — el is `content`. `\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\'` — descendant selector ✓.\\n\\nHmm, but hold on — the earlier failure cascade: tree test called `click(el, \'[data-toggle=\\\"行测\\\"]\')` — my click helper: `var node = el.querySelector(sel); if (!node) { failures.push(...); return null; }` — if node found, click. OK.\\n\\nWait, actually — maybe the real issue: in the questions test, `qcards.length !== 2` — no failure reported for that, so 2 cards found. Then toggle click... Let me look at the sequence again — actually, the click on the toggle: the toggle button is inside `[hidden]` container. In jsdom, `.click()` on a hidden element — jsdom dispatches the event; event.target.closest works. Should be OK.\\n\\nHmm — could it be that `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')` fails because toggleCard toggled `expanded` on the card but then... no.\\n\\nActually — WAIT. I see it. In my click handler:\\n\\n```js\\nlistBox.addEventListener(\'click\', function (e) {\\n  var btn = e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\');\\n  if (btn) {\\n    ...\\n    if (action === \'toggle\') { var c = btn.closest(\'.st-q-card\'); if (c) toggleCard(c); }\\n    ...\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  var card = e.target.closest(\'.st-q-card\');\\n  if (card && !e.target.closest(\'button\')) toggleCard(card);\\n});\\n```\\n\\nLooks right. Unless `e.target` is the SVG inside the button! In jsdom, icon() returns \'<svg></svg>\' — click on the BUTTON → target = button. But `node.click()` targets the button itself. OK.\\n\\nHmm, let me think about whether mount even succeeded — \\\"questions: 错题原因弹窗缺失\\\" means clicking `[data-action=\\\"wrong\\\"]` didn\'t call openWrongModal (modalState.html empty). But BEFORE that, the test clicked the q2 toggle → `[data-action=\\\"wrong\\\"]` is inside q2\'s hidden extra... if the FIRST toggle click (q1) failed to expand, that\'s just an assertion — the q2 toggle click still fires... The wrong click: `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q2\\\"] [data-action=\\\"wrong\\\"]\')` → openWrongModal(q2) → q2 exists ✓ → exists check ✓ → App.openModal called → modalState.html = reason options → check `modalState.html.indexOf(\'粗心\')` — should pass. It failed → openWrongModal NOT called → the click handler didn\'t fire or btn not found.\\n\\n\\\"批量导入弹窗缺失\\\" — click \'#st-q-import\' → openImportModal → modalState.html contains \'批量导入\' — failed → handler didn\'t fire.\\n\\nSo questions mount\'s event listeners aren\'t firing at all?? But `qcards.length === 2` passed (renderList populated cards). So mount ran and bound... unless mount didn\'t bind because it threw an exception mid-way BEFORE binding listBox click! Order in mount:\\n```js\\nvar listBox = el.querySelector(\'#st-q-list\');\\nvar focusId = pendingFocus; pendingFocus = \'\';\\nvar filters = {...};\\nfunction applyFilters...\\nfunction answerText...\\nfunction cardHTML...\\nfunction renderList...\\nfunction toggleCard...\\nlistBox.addEventListener(\'click\', ...)  ← binding\\nel.querySelector(\'#st-q-module\').addEventListener(\'change\', ...)\\n...\\nrenderList();\\n```\\nIf `el.querySelector(\'#st-q-module\')` returns null → TypeError → mount throws → router catches (\'页面 mount 出错\') but our test\'s mountPage calls def.mount directly → exception propagates → caught by my try/catch → failures.push(\'questions: \' + e.message)? But the reported failures don\'t include a \'questions:\' prefix exception... The try/catch would have added \'questions: <message>\'. Not present → no exception in questions mount. So listeners bound, renderList ran.\\n\\nHmm wait — but exceptions in EVENT HANDLERS are thrown asynchronously during click() — my try/catch around mountPage only covers mount call, not the click. So if listBox click handler throws (e.g., some null), the error would propagate up through node.click() → caught by my outer try/catch → recorded as \'questions: <msg>\'. Not present.\\n\\nLet me actually reproduce and debug instead of guessing. I\'ll add debug output. Actually — better idea: let me look at `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')` — my helper: `var node = el.querySelector(sel); if (!node) { failures.push(\'找不到元素: \' + sel); return null; }` — if node is null, it pushes \'找不到元素\' which would appear in failures — NOT present. So node found and clicked.\\n\\nHmm OK wait, maybe the problem is different: `card1 = el.querySelector(\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"]\')` — after toggle, `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')` false. Since the toggle click succeeded (no exception), toggleCard(card) ran: `card.classList.toggle(\'expanded\')`. classList.toggle in jsdom works...\\n\\nUnless `btn.closest(\'.st-q-card\')` returned null — btn is the toggle button inside .st-q-extra inside .st-q-card — closest works.\\n\\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the FIRST click: my click() calls node.click() — in jsdom, does click() on a button inside a `<div hidden>` fire? Yes.\\n\\nWait — maybe the issue is my icon() stub returns \'<svg></svg>\' — when innerHTML parsed, `.st-q-toggle` contains svg. The click on the button: e.target = button ✓.\\n\\nI\'m going in circles. Let me add debug output to harness3 to diagnose: after each major step, dump whether handlers fired. Actually, a much better approach: modify harness to log modalState.html snippets and card states. Let me add a debug mode: print detailed state on failure. Simpler: run a focused debug script that mounts the questions page and tries the toggle with instrumentation:\\n\\n```js\\n// debug: questions page\\ncontent.innerHTML = registered[\'/study/questions\'].render(\'/study/questions\');\\nvar spy = console.log;\\nregistered[\'/study/questions\'].mount(content, \'/study/questions\');\\nvar tog = content.querySelector(\'.st-q-card .st-q-toggle\');\\nconsole.log(\'tog found:\', !!tog, \'expanded before:\', content.querySelector(\'.st-q-card\').classList.contains(\'expanded\'));\\ntog.click();\\nconsole.log(\'expanded after:\', content.querySelector(\'.st-q-card\').classList.contains(\'expanded\'));\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait — actually! I just realized: `mountPage` helper for questions: `def.render(path || key)` — key=\'/study/questions\'... fine.\\n\\nOK — actually, let me reconsider something fundamental: does `el.querySelector(\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')` work in jsdom with the CSS attribute selector? Yes.\\n\\nHmm — one more idea: my mountPage does `content.innerHTML = def.render(...)` — render returns template with `${...}` interpolations — for questions page, the template includes `${modules.map(...)}` etc. — fine (render smoke passed, no ${ residuals).\\n\\nActually — WAIT. I bet I know what it is! The **toggle button contains an SVG** from icon(\'chevron\'). In `cardHTML`, the toggle is:\\n```html\\n<button class=\\\"st-q-toggle\\\" data-action=\\\"toggle\\\" data-id=\\\"...\\\">展开 ${icon(\'chevron\', 14)}</button>\\n```\\nicon stub returns \'<svg></svg>\'. Fine.\\n\\nHmm, but ALSO in the handler: `e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\')` — e.target = button ✓.\\n\\nOK let me stop guessing — instrument. Also fix the two known harness bugs (settings getter, review date 15, node re-query after refreshTree). And the batch import parse failure: \\\"可解析出 1 道题目，另有 1 条无法识别\\\" — my test input:\\n```\\n【资料分析】某市 GDP 增长 8%…\\nA. 5300 亿\\nB. 5400 亿\\nC. 5500 亿\\nD. 5600 亿\\n答案：B\\n解析：5000×1.08。\\n\\n第二题题干\\nA. x\\nB. y\\n答案：A\\n```\\nBlock 1: 【资料分析】 line → module=\'资料分析\'... wait the regex `/^【(.+?)】/` — line \'【资料分析】某市 GDP 增长 8%…\' matches → module=\'资料分析\'. But the stem lines: \'某市 GDP 增长 8%…\' is NOT captured — the whole line was consumed by the module regex (return). So stem = only... block 1 stemLines = [] → stem = \'\' → null → skipped! That\'s why \\\"1 道无法识别\\\". Hmm — this is a parser UX issue: `【模块】` at line start with stem text after it. My parser treats the whole line as module declaration. Real users would write:\\n```\\n【资料分析】\\n题干...\\n```\\nwith the stem on the NEXT line. But my test wrote stem on the same line. Real-world: both patterns are plausible. Fix parser: when `【...】` match, extract module and ALSO treat the remainder (after 】) as stem text if non-empty:\\n```js\\nif ((m = t.match(/^【(.+?)】(.*)$/))) { module = m[1].trim(); var rest = m[2].trim(); if (rest) stemLines.push(rest); return; }\\n```\\nThat\'s a genuine product improvement. Also block 2 \'第二题题干\' → stem ✓ → parsed. So with fix: 2 道, skipped 0.\\n\\nWait — but \\\"questions: 批量导入弹窗缺失\\\" — modalState.html didn\'t contain \'批量导入\'? My openImportModal html has `<p class=\\\"st-form-hint\\\">粘贴题目文本...` and title \'批量导入题目\'. Test checks `modalState.html.indexOf(\'批量导入\') < 0` — the html string is only the BODY (modal-head title is set by app.js openModal, not part of html param). So \'批量导入\' appears in body? The body contains \\\"批量导入\\\" only in... let me check: openImportModal html: `<p class=\\\"st-form-hint\\\">粘贴题目文本，多道题之间用空行分隔。...` — no \'批量导入\' text in body! The title \'批量导入题目\' is in opts.title (handled by app.js). So the test assertion is wrong — should check for \'题目文本\' or \'f-import-text\'. Test bug. Same for plan modal: test checks `modalState.html.indexOf(\'计划类型\')` — body contains \'计划类型\' label ✓ that one\'s fine (but it failed too — because the modal never opened → handler issue).\\n\\nAnd \\\"plan: 对比柱状图缺失\\\" — renderChart called in mount → box.innerHTML = bars — but wait, `renderChart(el.querySelector(\'#st-plan-chart\'), view)` — hmm, in PLAN_DEF.mount, after the auto-complete block: `if (changed) { App.refresh(); return; }` — changed=true (progress 35→58) → App.refresh() stub is NOOP → return → chart never rendered! TEST-ORDER issue: harness plan mount runs with changed=true → early return → chart missing. In the REAL app, App.refresh() re-renders the page properly. In harness, refresh is noop → mount aborted. That\'s why \'对比柱状图缺失\' AND \'计划详情展开失败\' AND \'新建计划弹窗缺失\' (no listeners bound — mount returned early).\\n\\nFix harness: make refresh() re-mount: `refresh: function(){ }` — I can implement: store current path & re-run mount? In harness, `window.App.refresh = function(){ currentRefresh && currentRefresh(); }` — set currentRefresh in mountPage to re-render the last mounted page. Simpler: in harness, `App.refresh` = function(){ if (lastMount) { content.innerHTML = lastDef.render(lastPath); lastDef.mount(content, lastPath); } }. But recursion: mount → changed → refresh → mount → changed false → proceed. OK safe. Let me implement refresh properly:\\n\\n```js\\nvar lastMount = null;\\nwindow.App.refresh = function(){ if (lastMount) { var m = lastMount; content.innerHTML = m.def.render(m.path); m.def.mount(content, m.path); } };\\nfunction mountPage(key, path){ var def = registered[key]; if(!def){...} content.innerHTML = def.render(path||key); lastMount = {def: def, path: path||key}; def.mount(content, path||key); return content; }\\n```\\n\\nCareful: App.refresh is referenced in mount BEFORE the click actions; fine.\\n\\nAlso note: plan mount `if (changed) { App.refresh(); return; }` — after refresh, the NEW mount runs; changed=false (progress already 58) → continues to bind ✓ chart rendered ✓.\\n\\nBut wait — in harness, planProgress uses checkins: c1 (today, 55min) & c2 (-1, 120min); p1 range: -1..+5 → 175min; target = 5h=300min → 58.33 → 58. p1.progress 35 ≠ 58 → update → changed=true → refresh. OK.\\n\\nNow \\\"wrong: Cannot read properties of null (reading \'click\')\\\" — at `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` probably? No wait, error message: \\\"wrong: Cannot read properties of null (reading \'click\')\\\" — that\'s my failures.push with e.message: \\\"Cannot read properties of null (reading \'click\')\\\" — where does a .click() on null happen? In my test: `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` — click helper pushes \'找不到元素\' if null... The error is THROWN somewhere: `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` returns null → `.click()` on null → TypeError → caught → failures.push(\'wrong: \' + e.message). So review panel didn\'t render options. Why? Because listBox click handler — `enterReview(\'w1\')` → ... `renderReview()` → reviewBox.innerHTML = panel with options → `reviewBox.querySelectorAll(\'[data-opt...]\')`... \\n\\nHmm — actually WAIT. Maybe the wrong page mount ALSO threw earlier? The tabs click `click(el, \'[data-tab=\\\"待复习\\\"]\')` worked? No failure for tab filter → items2.length===1 passed. Then \'全部\' tab click — no assertion. Then `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` — item found. enterReview: `var all = App.store.wrongQuestions.list().filter(...)` → w1,w2 → queue. reviewIdx=0. listBox.hidden=true; reviewBox.hidden=false; renderReview() → w=q1 → linked ✓ → reviewBox.innerHTML = panel... then `reviewBox.querySelectorAll(\'[data-review-exit]\').forEach(bind)` ✓ then option buttons bind click.\\n\\nThen test: `review = el.querySelector(\'#st-wrong-review\')` — review.hidden === false ✓ (no failure). `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` → should exist! Unless... the template\'s `${(q.options||[]).map(...)}` — q1 options 4 items ✓.\\n\\nHmm — unless enterReview was never called because the item click handler didn\'t fire (same symptom as questions!). Notice: questions handlers ALSO didn\'t fire. And tree refreshTree/refreshList worked (2 cards) but selection didn\'t apply (root cause = settings getter). And tree toggle \\\"无效\\\" — could ALSO be settings getter.\\n\\nQuestions: card expand failed — handler didn\'t fire. Wrong: enterReview — handler didn\'t fire. Plan: mount returned early (changed→refresh noop) — explained. Clock: settings getter explains initial state failures; submit \'未合并\' — clockState reads old settings → accumulated 0 → mins 0 → warn... but test expected success. After fixing settings getter, st = clockState() reads accumulated 61000 ✓ submit merges ✓.\\n\\nSo remaining mystery: questions & wrong click handlers didn\'t fire. Common denominator... OH WAIT. I think I see it now! In `mountPage`, I do `content.innerHTML = def.render(path || key)` then `def.mount(content, path || key)`. For questions, mount binds listBox listener ✓. Then test `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')`. el = content. Hmm.\\n\\nBUT — `el.querySelector(\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')` — the q1 card... wait, in renderList, cards generated with data-qid — q1 card exists (qcards.length 2 ✓).\\n\\nHmm, is it possible the click handler IS firing but `toggleCard` fails silently? `card.classList.toggle(\'expanded\')` — then `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')` false?? jsdom classList.toggle works...\\n\\nWait, actually — I wonder if the issue is that in jsdom, `node.click()` on a `<button>` element — does it work? jsdom implements HTMLElement.click() → dispatches a click MouseEvent ✓ (it\'s been supported for years).\\n\\nHmm, let me look at the questions mount code once more for an early exception BEFORE listBox binding... \\n\\n```js\\nmount: function (el) {\\n  var listBox = el.querySelector(\'#st-q-list\');\\n  var focusId = pendingFocus; pendingFocus = \'\';\\n  var filters = { module: \'\', tag: \'\', source: \'\', kw: \'\' };\\n  function applyFilters(list) {...}\\n  function answerText(q) {...}\\n  function cardHTML(q) {...}\\n  function renderList() {...}\\n  function toggleCard(card) {...}\\n  listBox.addEventListener(\'click\', function (e) {...});\\n  el.querySelector(\'#st-q-module\').addEventListener(\'change\', ...);\\n  el.querySelector(\'#st-q-tag\').addEventListener(\'change\', ...);\\n  el.querySelector(\'#st-q-source\').addEventListener(\'change\', ...);\\n  var searchInput = el.querySelector(\'#st-q-search\');\\n  ...\\n  el.querySelector(\'#st-q-add\').addEventListener(\'click\', ...);\\n  el.querySelector(\'#st-q-import\').addEventListener(\'click\', ...);\\n  renderList();\\n}\\n```\\nAll selectors exist in render (static check passed: 47 ids all resolvable — including #st-q-module etc. which are in the render output... wait, the static check #4 checked mount querySelector ids appear in the page\'s render output — it passed, so all good).\\n\\nHmm... so why wouldn\'t the click fire?! \\n\\nOH WAIT. I finally see it!!! In my click helper:\\n```js\\nfunction click(el, sel) {\\n  var node = el.querySelector(sel);\\n  if (!node) { failures.push(\'找不到元素: \' + sel); return null; }\\n  node.click();\\n  return node;\\n}\\n```\\n`node.click()` — for questions toggle → handler → toggleCard ✓. Then `card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\')`... \\n\\nHmm, hold on, let me check `toggleCard`:\\n```js\\nfunction toggleCard(card) {\\n  var expanded = card.classList.toggle(\'expanded\');\\n  var ex = card.querySelector(\'.st-q-extra\');\\n  if (ex) ex.hidden = !expanded;\\n}\\n```\\nclassList.toggle returns the NEW state ✓. expanded=true. ex.hidden=false. card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\') → TRUE. Should pass!\\n\\nUnless the handler never ran. Ugh. Let me just debug empirically with a focused script. I\'ll write a small debug harness that:\\n1. mounts questions page\\n2. adds a manual event listener log\\n3. clicks toggle\\n4. prints state\\n\\nActually — hmm, one MORE idea, and I think THIS is it: **`content.innerHTML = ...` then `def.mount(content, ...)`** — mount for questions... The router in real app does contentEl.innerHTML = html then mount. Fine.\\n\\nBUT WAIT — what about my `mountPage` — it\'s fine. What about the click on `.st-q-toggle` — the toggle button has `hidden`? No — the TOGGLE button is inside `.st-q-extra` which has the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE. When an element (or ancestor) has `hidden`, jsdom\'s click still dispatches. BUT — my handler: `var btn = e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\')` ✓... \\n\\nYou know what, let me just add a probe. Write debug-harness5 quickly. Actually — better: modify harness3\'s questions section to catch and print the actual exception. My try/catch already does `failures.push(\'questions: \' + e.message)` — but the click inside the try block would throw into that catch... and no such failure was recorded — meaning no exception, handler ran, but the assertion still failed?? If the handler ran and toggled, the assertion would pass. Contradiction... unless `click()` helper itself threw inside `node.click()` — that would be caught by the SAME try/catch → \'questions: ...\' failure recorded. Not present.\\n\\nWait wait. Let me recount the actual failure messages:\\n```\\nquestions: 卡片展开失败\\nquestions: 错题原因弹窗缺失\\nquestions: 批量导入弹窗缺失\\nquestions: 批量导入预览解析错误: 可解析出 1 道题目，另有 1 条无法识别\\nquestions: 批量导入未写入 store\\n```\\n\\\"卡片展开失败\\\" = `if (!card1.classList.contains(\'expanded\'))` — so no exception. Handler ran or not — no exception either way. So either handler didn\'t run (silently) or classList check failed.\\n\\nAnd \\\"错题原因弹窗缺失\\\" = `modalState.html.indexOf(\'粗心\') < 0` — hmm wait, but there\'s a subtlety: before clicking wrong, the test clicks q2\'s toggle first: `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q2\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')` (no assertion), then `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q2\\\"] [data-action=\\\"wrong\\\"]\')` → openWrongModal(\'q2\') → ... App.openModal called → modalState.html = options html → contains \'粗心\' ✓. Failed → openWrongModal didn\'t run or modalState not set.\\n\\n`App.openModal` — my stub: `openModal: function (html, opts) { var mask = document.createElement(\'div\'); mask.innerHTML = html; modalState.html = html; ... }` — document is jsdom\'s ✓. If openWrongModal ran, modalState.html set ✓.\\n\\nSo the handler chain didn\'t run for these clicks. Since no exceptions, the handler wasn\'t attached OR e.target.closest failed.\\n\\n**AH WAIT — I GOT IT!** `listBox.addEventListener(\'click\', ...)` — but the questions test\'s FIRST action is `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')`. But BEFORE that, in the TEST, we called... the tree test ended with `click(el, \'[data-toggle=\\\"行测\\\"]\')` on the TREE page. Then `mountPage(\'/study/questions\')` — new content. Fine.\\n\\nHmm, unless mountPage for questions THREW inside mount but the failure was swallowed?? My mountPage:\\n```js\\nfunction mountPage(key, path) {\\n  var def = registered[key];\\n  if (!def) { failures.push(key + \': 未注册\'); return null; }\\n  content.innerHTML = def.render(path || key);\\n  def.mount(content, path || key);\\n  return content;\\n}\\n```\\nNo try/catch here! If mount throws, it propagates to the outer try/catch in the test → \'questions: <e.message>\' recorded. Not present → mount didn\'t throw.\\n\\nOK — empirical debugging needed. Let me write a debug script (harness5) that instruments: after mounting questions, manually check if listBox has listeners by adding a capture-phase listener, and log results step by step. Also verify `e.target.closest` behavior.\\n\\nActually — hold on. One more theory, and I think it\'s very plausible: **`e.target.closest(\'[data-action]\')`** — in jsdom, `Element.prototype.closest` exists ✓.\\n\\nHmm, what about `listBox.querySelectorAll` etc... \\n\\nFINE. Empirical. Let me write debug harness. Actually, let me FIRST check one thing in the harness: the `click` helper for q1 toggle — selector `\'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\'`. In jsdom\'s querySelector, attribute selector `[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"]` — fine.\\n\\nDebug plan: after mounting questions, log: listBox exists, card count, toggle exists, then add a probe listener to listBox (capture) logging target & closest result; click toggle; log classList.\\n\\nLet me write it compactly and run in the same install+test call. I\'ll make harness5 minimal:\\n\\n```js\\n// same stubs as harness3 (settings getter fix!) + debug\\n```\\nRather than duplicate everything, I\'ll patch harness3 with the fixes AND add a debug probe section for questions. If probes reveal the issue, fix and rerun.\\n\\nFixes to harness3:\\n1. settings getter + updateSettings mutating a boxed object (keep reference stable):\\n```js\\nvar settingsBox = { theme: \'light\', fontSize: 15, sidebarCollapsed: false, studyUI: {}, studyClock: {} };\\nvar store = {\\n  settings: settingsBox,\\n  updateSettings: function (patch) { Object.keys(patch||{}).forEach(function(k){ settingsBox[k]=patch[k]; }); },\\n  ...\\n};\\n```\\nThis keeps store.settings === settingsBox always. And the clock test sets settingsBox.studyClock = {...}. Note the clock test currently does `settings.studyClock = {...}` — rename to settingsBox.\\n\\nHmm — but in real store.js, updateSettings REPLACES state.settings with a NEW object; store.settings property is read each time (live). My getter approach: store.settings as getter returning settingsBox — equivalent. Simpler: keep `settings: settingsBox` and mutate keys in place. But real code REPLACES (importJSON). For harness purposes in-place mutation is fine (test uses updateSettings).\\n\\n2. review date assertion: daysFromNow(15).\\n3. tree node re-query after toggle.\\n4. import modal assertion: check \'f-import-text\' instead of \'批量导入\'.\\n5. refresh implementation in App stub: re-mount last page.\\n6. Batch import parser fix in PRODUCT code (【模块】行尾带题干) — genuine product improvement. Apply to study.js.\\n\\nAlso fix in product: nothing else from these failures appears to be a product bug except the import parser (maybe). The wrong/questions handler mystery still needs diagnosis.\\n\\nAlso — for the plan test: with refresh implemented, mount → changed → refresh → re-mount (changed false) → proceed → chart rendered ✓.\\n\\nAlso the clock test\'s start/pause assertions: after fixing settingsBox, initial display 00:01:01 ✓; submit merges ✓ (uses st.accumulated from mount-time clockState — reads settingsBox.studyClock ✓).\\n\\nAnd sz test: `click(el, \'#st-sz-prev\')` → setYM → setUI → rerender → App.refresh → re-mounts with studyUI.szMonth persisted ✓ — the assertion checks settingsBox.studyUI.szMonth — with settingsBox fix, setUI writes to settingsBox.studyUI ✓.\\n\\nWait, sz assertion: `prevMonth = daysFromNow(-40).slice(0,7)` — today is 2026-08-03 (system date) → -40 days = 2026-06-24 → \'2026-06\'. shiftYM(\'2026-08\', -1) = \'2026-07\'. Test expects `settingsBox.studyUI.szMonth !== prevMonth` fail → expects \'2026-06\'?? WRONG — my test bug: after one prev click, month = \'2026-07\' not \'2026-06\'. prevMonth computed from s2\'s date (-40 days) is \'2026-06\'. The assertion `if (settings.studyUI.szMonth !== prevMonth)` — expects 2026-06 after ONE click — wrong; should be \'2026-07\'. Fix assertion: compare to shiftYM(current, -1) — compute current month \'2026-08\' → expect \'2026-07\'.\\n\\nAlso \\\"sz: 本月应显示 1 条，实际 0\\\" — with settingsBox fix, initial studyUI.szMonth undefined → ym = today slice(0,7)=\'2026-08\'; s1 date = today-3 → \'2026-07-31\'?? WAIT — today is 2026-08-03; -3 days = 2026-07-31 → s1.date.slice(0,7)=\'2026-07\' ≠ \'2026-08\' → s1 NOT in August! And s2 = -40 days = 2026-06-24 → \'2026-06\'. So in August there are ZERO items! My harness seed data is bad — s1 should be within the current month. daysFromNow(0) for s1 → today → \'2026-08-03\' ✓. Fix seed: s1 date = daysFromNow(0), s2 date = daysFromNow(-40).\\n\\nThen: August shows s1 (1 card) ✓; \'建议回顾\' tag for s1? s1 date = today → not 30 days ago → no tag. Test asserted `el.querySelector(\'.tag-warn\') === null` → fail message \'建议回顾标记缺失\' — with s1=today, no tag-warn → assertion `=== null` passes ✓. Good.\\n\\nAnd prev click → \'2026-07\' — s2 in \'2026-06\' — July empty... The test after prev click checks szMonth persisted ✓. Then tag filter \'民生\' — persisted ✓ (no list assertion). OK.\\n\\nAlso clock: `settings.studyClock = {...}` → change to settingsBox.studyClock.\\n\\nNow the mystery of questions/wrong handlers. Let me ALSO add probes. Actually — hmm, WAIT. I just thought of something. Let me re-examine the questions test flow with the OLD harness: BEFORE questions test, the tree test\'s LAST action was `click(el, \'[data-toggle=\\\"行测\\\"]\')`. With the settings getter bug, setUI created a NEW settings object; treeState() still read the OLD → no change → refreshTree no-op → openBefore===openAfter → \'tree: 折叠展开切换无效\' recorded. Then questions mount... \\n\\nHmm OK here\'s another thought — what if the questions mount\'s `renderList()` produced cards but the test\'s `click(el, \'.st-q-card[data-qid=\\\"q1\\\"] .st-q-toggle\')`... \\n\\nActually — hmm, actually let me look at the WRONG page failure more carefully: \\\"wrong: Cannot read properties of null (reading \'click\')\\\" — this is thrown INSIDE the try block, message = e.message. Which `.click()`? Candidates in the wrong section:\\n- `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` — inside, node.click() — node non-null (no \'找不到元素\').\\n- `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` — wait no, the test does `var optA = review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\'); if (!optA) { failures.push(\'wrong: 选项缺失\'); } optA.click();` — if optA null → \'wrong: 选项缺失\' pushed, then `optA.click()` → TypeError \'Cannot read properties of null (reading click)\' → caught → \'wrong: Cannot read...\'. So the failure message is the catch from the SAME line after pushing \'选项缺失\'?? But \'选项缺失\' wasn\'t in failures list! Hmm — because the catch pushes \'wrong: \' + e.message which OVERWRITES... no, both would be in the list. \'wrong: 选项缺失\' not present → optA was non-null?? Then which null.click()?\\n\\nLet me scan the wrong test section for other .click() calls:\\n- `w2opt.click()` — guarded by if (!w2opt) push...\\n- `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()` — NOT guarded!! After first review: `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` — my helper returns null if not found but pushes failure... the helper pushes \'找不到元素: #st-review-next\' — not in list. Hmm wait — in the wrong section, after the first answer I call `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')`. That\'s the helper — safe.\\n\\nThen: `click(review, \'[data-review-exit]\')` helper — safe.\\nThen `review = el.querySelector(\'#st-wrong-review\');` `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` — NOT guarded! If panel is the DONE panel (queue exhausted), there\'s no [data-opt=\\\"A\\\"] → null.click() → TypeError! \\n\\nSequence: w1 answered (A) → next → w2 (B wrong) → next → reviewIdx=2 → queue exhausted → DONE panel → then test clicks [data-review-exit] — wait, the test then does `click(review, \'[data-review-exit]\')` — review variable still points to OLD reviewBox? After `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` the panel re-rendered (done panel) — review element reference is the reviewBox (stable container) ✓. `[data-review-exit]` exists in done panel ✓ → exitReview → listBox shown, reviewBox cleared. Then `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` → w1 now status \'learning\' (from first answer) → enterReview ✓ → renderReview → options ✓ → `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()`... \\n\\nwait — `review = el.querySelector(\'#st-wrong-review\')` — re-queried ✓. Panel shows w1 again? Queue = [w1, w2] (both non-archived). reviewIdx=0. Click A → correct → consecutive 2 → status learning, nextReview +4d. Then `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()` — hmm the test does `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()`?? Let me re-read my test:\\n\\n```js\\nclick(review, \'#st-review-next\');\\nreview.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click();\\nvar w1b = store.wrongQuestions.get(\'w1\');\\nif (w1b.status !== \'archived\') failures.push(...)\\n```\\nAfter first A (consecutive 2), click next → reviewIdx=1 → w2 rendered (options A,B) → `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` — w2 options exist → click A → correct → consecutive 2 → learning. Then check w1b.status — w1 has consecutive 2 — NOT archived → failure \'连续答对 3 次未归档\'... \\n\\nHmm wait — w1\'s consecutive was already 1 from the FIRST round (before exiting)! In round 1: w1 answered A (consecutive 1), then w2 answered B wrong (consecutive 0). Then exit. Round 2: w1 answered A → consecutive 2, next → w2 answered A → correct! consecutive 1. Then w1b.status = learning (consecutive 2) → NOT archived → failure recorded? But \'连续答对 3 次未归档\' is NOT in the failure list! Because the null.click() TypeError happened BEFORE that check — at `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` when panel showed the DONE state.\\n\\nLet me recount: after w2 answered wrong (B) in round 1: `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` → reviewIdx=2 → done panel. Then test: `click(review, \'[data-review-exit]\')` — done panel HAS data-review-exit ✓ → exit. Then `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` → enterReview(w1) → queue=[w1,w2] → render w1 → `review = el.querySelector(\'#st-wrong-review\')` → panel with options → `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` ✓ → correct → consecutive 2 → next → `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\')`... wait after clicking A, result panel has #st-review-next; test does `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()` — hmm no — let me look at exact test code:\\n\\n```js\\nclick(review, \'#st-review-next\');     // helper — safe\\nreview.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click();   // ← panel now w2 (after next)\\nvar w1b = store.wrongQuestions.get(\'w1\');\\nif (w1b.status !== \'archived\') failures.push(\'wrong: 连续答对 3 次未归档...\');\\nif (review.querySelector(\'#st-review-result\').textContent.indexOf(\'已自动归档\') < 0) failures.push(...)\\nif (review.querySelector(\'.st-confetti\') === null) failures.push(...)\\n```\\nAfter `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` → reviewIdx=1 → w2 rendered. `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` → exists (w2 options) → click → correct → w2 consecutive 2 → learning — result panel \'答对了！\' not \'已自动归档\'... Then w1b.status: w1 consecutive 2 → NOT archived → \'连续答对 3 次未归档\' SHOULD be in failures. It\'s NOT.\\n\\nSo the flow differs — meaning an EARLIER step threw. The catch would record \'wrong: <msg>\'. The recorded message: \\\"wrong: Cannot read properties of null (reading \'click\')\\\" — that\'s the FIRST thrown error in the wrong section. Where? \\n\\nPossibilities:\\n- `optA.click()` after `if (!optA) failures.push(\'wrong: 选项缺失\')` — optA null → push \'选项缺失\' AND then optA.click() throws → catch pushes \'wrong: Cannot read...\'. But \'wrong: 选项缺失\' would ALSO be in failures. It\'s NOT.\\n\\nSo optA was non-null. Then where\'s null.click()? Hmm... `w2opt.click()` — guarded similarly. `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()` — hmm wait, in the result panel, the button id is \'st-review-next\' ✓ exists... but the test line is `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` (helper) — safe.\\n\\nHmm, what about inside the first block:\\n```js\\nvar optA = review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\');\\nif (!optA) failures.push(\'wrong: 选项缺失\');\\noptA.click();\\n```\\noptA non-null (no \'选项缺失\'). Then result panel → `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-result\')` → `result.textContent.indexOf(\'答对了\')` — passed (no failure recorded). Then w1 checks — passed?? w1.status learning, consecutive 1 ✓ nextReview +2 ✓ — no failures recorded for those. Then `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` → w2 → `w2opt = review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"B\\\"]\')` — exists (q2 has A,B) → click B → WRONG (answer A) → status pending, consecutive 0, nextReview +1 ✓ — those assertions passed (no failures). Then `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` → reviewIdx=2 → done panel. `click(review, \'[data-review-exit]\')` → exit. `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` → enterReview — listBox re-rendered? exitReview calls renderList() — re-queries? NO! renderList uses `items.map(itemHTML)` fresh from store ✓ — w1 item still exists ✓. click w1 → enterReview → queue [w1, w2] (w1 learning, w2 pending) → reviewIdx 0 → renderReview... \\n\\n`review = el.querySelector(\'#st-wrong-review\')` — panel now contains w1 question. `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` → correct → consecutive 2 → next → w2 → `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` → w2 correct → consecutive 2... \\n\\nWAIT — I need to recount w2\'s consecutive: round 1: w2 answered B (wrong) → consecutive 0. Round 2 (current): w2 answered A → consecutive 1. So w1b (w1) = consecutive 2, status \'learning\' → assertion \'连续答对 3 次未归档\' FAILS → should be recorded... NOT in list!\\n\\nSo the actual flow differs from my mental model — the exception happened earlier. The only unguarded .click() calls in the wrong section: `optA.click()`, `w2opt.click()`, `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` (in the archived check section), `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-next\').click()` — the last two! Let me recount the archived-check section:\\n\\n```js\\nclick(review, \'#st-review-next\');\\nreview.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click();\\n```\\nIf `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` returned null (button not found), my helper pushes \'找不到元素: #st-review-next\' — NOT in failures... unless the button WAS found. Then `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\')` — if panel = done panel → null → TypeError → \'wrong: Cannot read properties of null (reading click)\' ✓✓✓ MATCHES!\\n\\nSo after round-2 w1 answer (consecutive 2) → `click(review, \'#st-review-next\')` → reviewIdx=1 → w2... wait — queue is [w1, w2]; reviewIdx was 0 (w1), next → 1 (w2) — renders w2 with options — [data-opt=\\\"A\\\"] exists...\\n\\nUNLESS the queue doesn\'t contain w2! enterReview builds queue: `App.store.wrongQuestions.list().filter(w => w.status !== \'archived\')` — w1 (learning), w2 (pending after round-1 wrong) → both → queue=[w1,w2]. Hmm.\\n\\nOR unless after round-1\'s w2-wrong answer, my assertion `w2.nextReview !== daysFromNow(1)` etc — those passed (not in failures).\\n\\nHmm OK wait, maybe the flow broke EARLIER: after round-1 w1 answer, I check `review.querySelector(\'#st-review-result\').textContent.indexOf(\'答对了\')` — passed. But hmm — \'答对了\' with consecutive 1: banner = \'答对了！...\' ✓.\\n\\nI clearly need empirical debugging instead of mental simulation. Let me add detailed step logging to the wrong test section temporarily... Actually, better: let me just fix the harness issues I\'ve identified (settings getter, refresh, seeds, assertions) and add a few defensive guards, then re-run and see what remains. The remaining failures will then be diagnosable.\\n\\nActually — one more important realization about the WRONG page: `enterReview` is triggered by clicking `[data-review]` in listBox. In round 2, test clicks `[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]` — but wait, after exitReview + renderList, is the element still in DOM? Yes.\\n\\nBUT — hmm, actually — I wonder if `click(el, \'[data-review=\\\"w1\\\"]\')` in round 2 actually re-entered review, or if the CLICK hit the element but enterReview found w1 status \'learning\'... enterReview doesn\'t filter status (queue filters archived only) ✓.\\n\\nOK let me also double check one thing in the wrong round-2 expectations: my test after round-2 w1 answer expects... it does NOT assert w1 status there; it asserts after clicking next: `review.querySelector(\'[data-opt=\\\"A\\\"]\').click()` then w1b.status===\'archived\' expecting 3 consecutive. For w1: round1 answer1 → consecutive 1; round2 answer1 → consecutive 2; the THIRD A click (in the archived section) → consecutive 3 → archived ✓ — the test clicks A once more after next → that\'s w2\'s A → consecutive 1 for w2, w1 stays 2. So w1b.status === \'learning\' → \'连续答对 3 次未归档\' failure expected... STILL doesn\'t match observations.\\n\\nI\'m clearly mis-tracing. EMPIRICAL. Let me rewrite harness3 sections with more instrumentation & the fixes, then run and see. Given the volume of changes, I\'ll rewrite harness3 entirely (cleaner) with:\\n- settings box fix\\n- refresh re-mount\\n- scrollIntoView polyfill\\n- corrected seeds (s1 = today, s2 = -40d)\\n- corrected assertions (15 days, sz month \'2026-07\', import body check \'f-import-text\')\\n- tree re-query after toggle\\n- guarded clicks with helper + explicit failure messages\\n- add step logging to wrong section (console.log milestones) so I can see where it diverges\\n- questions: add probe: after mount, log listener state? I\'ll add console.log of card count + a capture probe on listBox before clicking (listener added via listBox.addEventListener(\'click\', ..., true) logging target) — hmm, simpler: log after each action.\\n\\nAlso fix the product import parser (【模块】same-line stem). Let me apply the product fix first.\",\"content\":\"jsdom 跑通了，失败中大部分是**测试脚手架自身缺陷**（store 桩的 `settings` 引用未随 updateSettings 更新、`App.refresh` 桩为空操作、断言日期算错、种子数据月份不对），但批量导入解析暴露了一个真实产品问题：`【模块】题干同行` 时题干被吞掉。先修产品解析器，再重写测试脚手架。\",\"content_discarded\":false,\"summary\":\"jsdom 跑通了，失败中大部分是**测试脚手架自身缺陷**（store 桩的 `settings` 引用未随 updateSettings 更新、`App.refresh` 桩为空操作、断言日期算错、种子数据月份不对），但批量导入解析暴露了一个真实产品问题：`【模块】题干同行` 时题干被吞掉。先修产品解析器，再重写测试脚手架。\",\"metadata\":{\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"iteration\":56,\"round\":56,\"phase\":\"execution\",\"finish_reason\":\"tool_calls\"},\"round\":56,\"phase\":\"execution\",\"finish_reason\":\"tool_calls\"}', '2026-08-03 14:39:01'),
(1454, '40122540-fcbb-4890-9b07-1296c60efc8f', 190466, 'tool.started', 'code', 'moonya', 'pages_study', 'call_00_rLiQYF84hqs0iWtqQhgF8962', '{\"tool_key\":\"edit_file\",\"display_name\":\"Edit File\",\"arguments\":{\"command\":\"str_replace\",\"new_str\":\"      if ((m = t.match(/^【(.+?)】(.*)$/))) { module = m[1].trim(); if (m[2].trim()) stemLines.push(m[2].trim()); return; }\",\"old_str\":\"      if ((m = t.match(/^【(.+?)】/))) { module = m[1].trim(); return; }\",\"path\":\"D:\\\\Project\\\\Project\\\\ccee\\\\web1\\\\js\\\\pages\\\\study.js\"},\"effect\":\"write\",\"risk_level\":\"medium\",\"phase\":null,\"operation_id\":null,\"intent\":null,\"shell\":null,\"command_fingerprint\":null}', '2026-08-03 14:39:01'),
